    "mini": "<svg viewBox='0 0 120 64' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' aria-hidden='true'><line x1='44' y1='8' x2='44' y2='56' stroke='#8FA6AD' stroke-width='1.4' stroke-dasharray='3 3'/><path d='M44,22 L20,22' stroke='#0E4D5C' stroke-width='2.6'/><polygon points='20,17 12,22 20,27' fill='#1C8C9B'/><path d='M44,44 L100,44' stroke='#993C1D' stroke-width='2.6'/><polygon points='100,39 108,44 100,49' fill='#E2664A'/></svg>",
    "svg": "<svg viewBox='0 0 760 488' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Two long run estimates with their ranges. Remittances per person lower the top one per cent income share by 0.278 per cent. Income produced per person raises it by 2.651 per cent. Both ranges stay clear of zero.'><rect width='760' height='488' fill='#F6FBFB'/><text x='40' y='42' font-family='Fraunces, Georgia, serif' font-size='19' fill='#0B2530'>Remittances narrow the gap, growth widens it faster</text><text x='40' y='62' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#3D5560'>Long run effect on the share of Jamaican income held by the richest one per cent, 2010 to 2019</text><text x='40' y='102' font-family='Space Mono, monospace' font-size='9.5' letter-spacing='1.5' fill='#3D5560'>LONG RUN EFFECT, WITH THE RANGE THE DATA ALLOW</text><line x1='335.5' y1='118' x2='335.5' y2='268' stroke='#8FA6AD' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-dasharray='4 4'/><text x='335.5' y='114' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#8FA6AD'>no effect</text><text x='40' y='158' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#0B2530'>Remittances received per person</text><line x1='298.5' y1='154' x2='324.9' y2='154' stroke='#0E4D5C' stroke-width='2.2'/><line x1='298.5' y1='146' x2='298.5' y2='162' stroke='#0E4D5C' stroke-width='2.2'/><line x1='324.9' y1='146' x2='324.9' y2='162' stroke='#0E4D5C' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='311.7' cy='154' r='6' fill='#1C8C9B' stroke='#0E4D5C' stroke-width='1.8'/><text x='286.5' y='159' text-anchor='end' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#0E4D5C'>-0.278</text><text x='40' y='224' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#0B2530'>Income produced per person</text><line x1='435.9' y1='220' x2='688.1' y2='220' stroke='#993C1D' stroke-width='2.2'/><line x1='435.9' y1='212' x2='435.9' y2='228' stroke='#993C1D' stroke-width='2.2'/><line x1='688.1' y1='212' x2='688.1' y2='228' stroke='#993C1D' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='562.0' cy='220' r='6' fill='#E2664A' stroke='#993C1D' stroke-width='1.8'/><text x='700.1' y='225' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#993C1D'>+2.651</text><line x1='250' y1='268' x2='720' y2='268' stroke='#3D5560' stroke-width='1.6'/><line x1='250.0' y1='268' x2='250.0' y2='274' stroke='#3D5560' stroke-width='1.4'/><text x='250.0' y='290' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#3D5560'>-1</text><line x1='335.5' y1='268' x2='335.5' y2='274' stroke='#3D5560' stroke-width='1.4'/><text x='335.5' y='290' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#3D5560'>+0</text><line x1='420.9' y1='268' x2='420.9' y2='274' stroke='#3D5560' stroke-width='1.4'/><text x='420.9' y='290' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#3D5560'>+1</text><line x1='506.4' y1='268' x2='506.4' y2='274' stroke='#3D5560' stroke-width='1.4'/><text x='506.4' y='290' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#3D5560'>+2</text><line x1='591.8' y1='268' x2='591.8' y2='274' stroke='#3D5560' stroke-width='1.4'/><text x='591.8' y='290' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#3D5560'>+3</text><line x1='677.3' y1='268' x2='677.3' y2='274' stroke='#3D5560' stroke-width='1.4'/><text x='677.3' y='290' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11' fill='#3D5560'>+4</text><text x='430' y='310' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#3D5560'>change in the top one per cent share, in per cent, for every one per cent more of each</text><text x='40' y='344' font-family='Space Mono, monospace' font-size='9.5' letter-spacing='1.5' fill='#3D5560'>THE SAME TWO NUMBERS, WORKED THROUGH</text><text x='40' y='366' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#0B2530'>Over these ten years the richest one per cent held 19.7 per cent of all income before tax.</text><circle cx='47' cy='389' r='6' fill='#1C8C9B' stroke='#0E4D5C' stroke-width='1.6'/><text x='62' y='394' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#0B2530'>Ten per cent more sent home per person moves that share to 19.1, a fall of half a point.</text><circle cx='47' cy='414' r='6' fill='#E2664A' stroke='#993C1D' stroke-width='1.6'/><text x='62' y='419' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#0B2530'>Ten per cent more income produced per person moves it to 24.9, a rise of five points.</text><line x1='40' y1='436' x2='720' y2='436' stroke='#D7E6E6' stroke-width='2'/><text x='380' y='460' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#3D5560'>Ten years of annual figures, stretched to forty quarterly points by the authors before estimation.</text><path d='M0,472 C150,466 320,477 480,472 C610,468 700,475 760,472 L760,488 L0,488 Z' fill='#BFE3E2' opacity='0.55'/></svg>",
    "note": "Adian McFarlane, Leanora Brown and Anupam Das ask what remittances do to the way income is shared out in Jamaica, rather than to how much of it there is. They measure the gap between rich and poor as the share of national income before tax held by the richest one per cent, which averaged 19.7 per cent between 2010 and 2019. Of their three long run estimates the two attached to money point opposite ways, and urbanisation cuts the top share by more than remittances do. Ten per cent more remittances per person goes with that share falling by about half a percentage point, and ten per cent more income produced per person goes with it rising by about five points. The estimate attached to growth is roughly nine and a half times the size of the one attached to remittances, and it points the other way. The estimates rest on ten annual observations, which the authors stretch to forty quarterly points by interpolation before fitting the model, so the sample is thinner than the number of data points suggests, and they say plainly that the finding is about Jamaica rather than about remittance receiving countries in general.",
