    "title": "Two lifelines, one economy",
    "paper": "Remittances and economic growth in East Africa (Adow, 2025)",
    "mini": "<svg viewBox='0 0 120 64' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' aria-hidden='true'><circle cx='46' cy='32' r='25' fill='#1C8C9B' fill-opacity='0.50'/><circle cx='78' cy='32' r='22' fill='#E2664A' fill-opacity='0.45'/><text x='62' y='38' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Fraunces, Georgia, serif' font-size='16' fill='#0B2530'>−</text></svg>",
    "svg": "<svg viewBox='0 0 760 520' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' role='img' aria-label='Two overlapping circles. Remittances lift growth by about half a point for each extra point of GDP. Aid also lifts growth. Where the two arrive together the combined lift is smaller than the sum. A panel of East African countries including Comoros.'><rect width='760' height='520' fill='#F6FBFB'/><text x='40' y='42' font-family='Fraunces, Georgia, serif' font-size='19' fill='#0B2530'>Two lifelines, one economy</text><text x='40' y='62' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#3D5560'>remittances, aid and growth in an East African panel including Comoros: Adow, 2025</text><text x='40' y='80' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='12.5' fill='#3D5560'>15 countries, 2000 to 2022, with Comoros among the region's largest receivers of remittances</text><text x='312.0' y='104' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Space Mono, monospace' font-size='11' letter-spacing='2' fill='#0E4D5C'>REMITTANCES</text><text x='498.0' y='104' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Space Mono, monospace' font-size='11' letter-spacing='2' fill='#993C1D'>AID (ODA)</text><circle cx='340' cy='218' r='100' fill='#1C8C9B' fill-opacity='0.50'/><circle cx='470' cy='218' r='86' fill='#E2664A' fill-opacity='0.45'/><text x='312.0' y='229' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Fraunces, Georgia, serif' font-size='32' fill='#0E4D5C'>+0.5</text><text x='498.0' y='229' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Fraunces, Georgia, serif' font-size='32' fill='#993C1D'>+</text><text x='412.0' y='229' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Fraunces, Georgia, serif' font-size='34' fill='#0B2530'>−</text><rect x='40' y='336' width='216' height='94' rx='5' fill='#1C8C9B' fill-opacity='0.12' stroke='#0E4D5C' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='54' y='361' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#0E4D5C'>+0.5 points</text><text x='54' y='383' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#0B2530'>each extra point of GDP</text><text x='54' y='398' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#0B2530'>arriving as remittances lifts</text><text x='54' y='413' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#0B2530'>growth by about half a point</text><rect x='272' y='336' width='216' height='94' rx='5' fill='#E2664A' fill-opacity='0.12' stroke='#993C1D' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='286' y='361' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#993C1D'>aid helps too</text><text x='286' y='383' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#0B2530'>in the same panel, aid</text><text x='286' y='398' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#0B2530'>arriving on its own also</text><text x='286' y='413' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#0B2530'>raises growth</text><rect x='504' y='336' width='216' height='94' rx='5' fill='#8FA6AD' fill-opacity='0.14' stroke='#0B2530' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='518' y='361' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13.5' fill='#0B2530'>but not both</text><text x='518' y='383' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#0B2530'>where the two arrive together,</text><text x='518' y='398' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#0B2530'>the combined lift is smaller</text><text x='518' y='413' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='11.5' fill='#0B2530'>than the sum</text><line x1='36' y1='452' x2='724' y2='452' stroke='#D7E6E6' stroke-width='2'/><text x='380' y='474' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#3D5560'>where both arrive at once, the combined effect falls short of the sum</text><text x='380' y='492' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Spline Sans, sans-serif' font-size='13' fill='#3D5560'>how the two work together on the ground is what this project goes to Comoros to find out</text><path d='M0,500 C140,493 300,504 460,499 C600,494 690,502 760,498 L760,520 L0,520 Z' fill='#BFE3E2' opacity='0.55'/></svg>",
    "note": "Across a panel of 15 East African countries from 2000 to 2022, with Comoros among the region's top remittance recipients, each extra point of GDP arriving as remittances is associated with about half a point more growth per person, a result that survives the toughest specification in the paper. In the fixed-effects models aid raises growth too, and the striking result sits in the overlap, where the interaction between remittances and aid is negative and significant at the 1 per cent level, though both of those weaken once the paper corrects for endogeneity. The negative interaction means meaning that where both flows arrive together their combined effect falls short of the sum of the parts, perhaps through overlapping allocations or conflicting conditions. That interaction, tested on the ground rather than in a panel, is precisely the question FAR-SIDS takes to Jamaica, Tonga, and Comoros. Read in full through the journal's open access text.",
